# Making repair unconditional is caught by the EARLIEST plain run in the file,
# not by the byte-comparison written for it further down, and the `m` says so on
# purpose. Repairing by default changes the VERDICT everywhere: every plain
# `doc` call reports REPAIRED instead of STALE, so the first assertion about
# a stale verdict dies before any later one about bytes can be reached. There
# is no mutation that reaches the later one while the earlier assertion exists,
# which makes the byte check a deliberately redundant second statement of the
# property rather than the guard that holds it up.
